@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON document supplied as text, with syntax highlighting. The output is the JSON re-serialized over multiple lines, indented by 4 spaces by default. Pass ``indent`` to change the number of spaces, and ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output is available by logging a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. Import ``JSON`` from ``rich.json``::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('[false, true, null, "foo"]'))
+
+The package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function behaves the same way, and uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+JSON output disables word wrapping, but default printing still crops lines wider than the console. To keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping, for example ``console.print(JSON(text), soft_wrap=True)``.
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line by running the json module with the path to a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
